    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d444">

                <pb facs="0541" n="445" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg445"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Iddings to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Rome, Italy</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-07-29"><hi rendition="#italic">July 29,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 477.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I beg leave to inclose herewith a clipping,
                    with translation, from L’ltalie, a daily newspaper printed in Rome in French,
                    and another from the daily newspaper La Tribuna, also published in Rome. That
                    from L’ltalie is of the date of the 25th instant, and that from La Tribuna of
                    the 24th. Both refer to the lynchings at Tallulah and represent fairly public
                    opinion which gets into print. In private conversation Italians seem to
                    understand pretty well how such incidents occur.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lewis Morris
                    Iddings.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d444-a1.xml" xpointer="d444-a1"/>
